{
  "campaign": "c15-llama-b12032",
  "notes": [
    "llama.cpp b11925 -> b12032 (release 0.8.6, and 0.8.7 with the chat UI port fix), as",
    "docs/llama-bump-b12032.md asks of LUMI-G: the same points on 0.8.5 (a3bbe56, b11925, label",
    "v085) and on 0.8.7 (label v087), round r-b12032, sized for jobs of 4 hours. The v085 half",
    "runs in r-v085's chains, ahead of that round's points; v087 has a chain of its own.",
    "b12-moe first, the whole node: the 235B's expert cache at 8 and 32 requests with",
    "LLAMA_MOE_CACHE_STATS=64 (0002 and 0008 apply as they were).",
    "b12-tensor: Qwen3-32B with --split-mode tensor on four GCDs, beside the other points (not",
    "alone, to keep the test to one job): 0012 was regenerated on upstream's new loader.",
    "b12-mtp: the six Qwen3.5/3.6 MTP twins without --mtp and with --mtp 2, 100 gsm8k questions",
    "at temperature 0: 0013 beside upstream's ballot on ROCm 6 (#31259), and #31368's stricter",
    "rollbacks of recurrent memory, which --mtp 2 should not meet (no error in the log).",
    "b12-soak-*: two to two and a half hours at 32 requests on one GCD, llama.cpp's log on: no",
    "server death, no HSA error, no out of memory, no NaN (tools/lumi/log_checks.py).",
    "b12-pool-flush: the same soak with GGML_CUDA_POOL_KEEP_FREE_MB=40000, so that the pool",
    "flushes (0011): with the defaults the soaks never came near full VRAM and the flush has",
    "never run on ROCm. Its speed is not a result: a stress of the flush, 'pool[...] freeing'",
    "lines expected, and nothing else in the log.",
    "b12-dsa, b12-dsa-graded: DeepSeek-V4-Flash (284B, 13B active, architecture deepseek4, MIT),",
    "unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL (155 GB) on four GCDs. Its sparse attention runs on CDNA from b12032",
    "(#31259: the sparse flash-attention kernel, made for 64-wide wavefronts); 0.8.5 reads it",
    "with the dense kernel and its mask. Reading and writing at 0, 16,000 and 30,000 tokens of",
    "prompt, and 100 gsm8k questions at temperature 0 for the same answers on both binaries."
  ],
